Manufacturing Process Security & Anomaly Detection
Overview
This module implements the Manufacturing Process Security Layer of SecurePrint AI. Its purpose is to continuously monitor the additive manufacturing process, detect malicious modifications, identify abnormal printer behavior, and automatically respond to security incidents before defective or unsafe parts are produced.
The system combines four complementary security mechanisms:
1. G-code Security Analysis — Detects malicious or unsafe commands inside print files.
2. Statistical Process Control (SPC) — Detects process drift and quality degradation.
3. Isolation Forest Anomaly Detection — Identifies unusual telemetry patterns using machine learning.
4. SIEM-Based Incident Response — Logs security events and triggers automated mitigation actions.
Together, these components provide real-time protection against cyberattacks, sabotage, configuration tampering, and manufacturing defects.
________________


5.1 G-Code Security Analyzer
Purpose
Before a print begins, the platform analyzes the uploaded G-code file to determine whether it contains unsafe instructions, modified parameters, or signs of tampering.
This is important because an attacker can manipulate G-code without changing the visual appearance of the CAD model.
Examples include:
* Increasing nozzle temperature to dangerous levels
* Modifying feed rates
* Resetting printer firmware settings
* Triggering unexpected machine behavior
* Corrupting production quality
________________


Safe Operating Ranges
The analyzer defines acceptable operating limits for common materials.
SAFE_RANGES = {
    "PLA":  (175, 230),
    "ABS":  (220, 260),
    "PETG": (220, 250),
    "TPU":  (200, 240)
}
These limits act as baseline security policies.
If a command exceeds these thresholds, the file is flagged.
________________


Dangerous Command Detection
The system maintains a list of high-risk G-code commands.
DANGEROUS_COMMANDS = [
    "M303",
    "SAVE_CONFIG",
    "M500",
    "M502",
    "FIRMWARE_RESTART"
]
Examples:
M303
PID auto-tuning can alter thermal behavior and potentially cause overheating.
SAVE_CONFIG
Can permanently modify printer configuration.
M500
Writes settings to EEPROM.
FIRMWARE_RESTART
Restarts firmware during operation and may disrupt production.
________________


File Integrity Verification
The analyzer calculates a SHA-256 hash.
actual_hash = hashlib.sha256(content.encode()).hexdigest()
The hash is compared against the expected signed version.
If they differ:
integrity_ok = False
A FILE_TAMPERED alert is generated.
________________


Temperature Security Checks
The system searches for commands such as:
M104 S350
Meaning:
Set nozzle temperature to 350°C
Because SecurePrint AI enforces a maximum safe temperature of 300°C:
if temp > 300:
the command is classified as critical.
Possible consequences:
* Fire risk
* Material degradation
* Equipment damage
________________


Feed Rate Validation
The analyzer extracts motion commands:
G1 X100 Y100 F20000
and validates:
feed_rate < 15000 mm/min
Excessive speeds may cause:
* Layer shifting
* Structural defects
* Mechanical stress
________________


Risk Score Calculation
The analyzer computes a cumulative risk score.
risk_score =
critical_alerts × 0.4
+ high_alerts × 0.2
+ violations × 0.15
Output range:
0.0 → Safe
1.0 → Critical
________________


Recommendation Engine
Based on risk score:
Risk Score
	Action
	< 0.2
	SAFE_TO_PRINT
	0.2 – 0.5
	PROCEED_WITH_CAUTION
	> 0.5
	BLOCK_PRINT
	This prevents unsafe files from reaching production.
________________


5.2 Statistical Process Control (SPC)
Purpose
Even when a G-code file is safe, the manufacturing process itself may gradually drift.
Examples:
* Temperature instability
* Calibration degradation
* Sensor inaccuracies
* Mechanical wear
SPC continuously evaluates process stability.
________________


Rolling Window Monitoring
The controller stores recent measurements.
self.history[parameter]
Examples:
Nozzle Temperature
Bed Temperature
Flow Rate
Extrusion Rate
A moving window of measurements is analyzed.
________________


Rule 1 — Three Sigma Rule
abs(value - mean) > 3 * std
Meaning:
A value lies more than three standard deviations away from normal operation.
Example:
Normal = 200°C
Observed = 215°C
This indicates an outlier.
Severity:
HIGH
________________


Rule 2 — Nine Consecutive Points
If nine measurements remain on the same side of the mean:
all(x > mean)
the process is drifting.
Example:
200
201
202
203
204
205
206
207
208
This may indicate:
* Heater degradation
* Sensor bias
* Calibration issues
Severity:
MEDIUM
________________


Rule 3 — Six Consecutive Trends
The controller detects monotonic behavior.
Example:
200
201
202
203
204
205
This indicates an upward trend.
Potential causes:
* Thermal runaway
* Hardware degradation
* Environmental changes
________________


Process Capability Index (Cpk)
The system evaluates manufacturing capability.
Formula:
Cpk = min(CPU, CPL)
Interpretation:
Cpk
	Meaning
	>1.67
	Six Sigma
	>1.33
	Capable
	>1.0
	Marginal
	<1.0
	Incapable
	This metric is widely used in aerospace and medical manufacturing.
